NAHPU is optimized for mobile and desktop screens and is designed to record natural history specimens both in the lab and in the field.

## Key Concepts

### Understanding Projects

NAHPU manages and organizes data by project. A project can be a field expedition, a collection of specimens prepared in a lab for a given year, or a long-term monitoring project. You can think of a project as a "folder" that contains all the data and media for a specific research effort. Each project has its own unique identifier (UUID) and can be managed independently. Search, export, import, and data statistics are currently managed at the project level. This approach ensures that data from a single expedition or research effort stays together and remains easy to find. If your institution uses accession numbers, you can assign one to a project or use the same accession number across multiple projects.

{% aside type="note" %}
Future NAHPU updates will provide global search, export, and data statistics across multiple projects.
{% /aside %}

### Site and Collecting Event Relationships

If you are coming from a paper-based catalog, this may be a new concept: think of a collecting event as the "session" during which specimens were collected, separate from the site where it happened. A site contains geographic information, habitat conditions, and associated coordinates and habitats—in other words, information that usually does not change over a short period. A collecting event, on the other hand, captures details that vary at a specific site and during a specific period, such as the personnel involved, trap counts, weather, and astronomy data. It captures the effort and associated environmental conditions at that time. Specimen records link to a collecting event rather than directly to a site.

### Location, Locality, and Site Scopes

These three terms are often used interchangeably within the museum community. The terms location, locality, and site—and the ontologies behind them—can lead to [an "exhausting" debate](https://naomicleghorn.weebly.com/blog/site-vs-locality-and-the-paleocore-workshop). NAHPU reserves location for a broad place scoped to the whole project. These location details are recorded in the project information. Throughout the app, NAHPU uses the term Site for a more specific and narrower scope than locality. The camp, the hotel where you stay during an expedition, a net or trap line, and so on are all sites. You can add any number of coordinates to a single site. For example, when collecting specimens with traps, you can record a coordinate for each trap and assign a specimen to that coordinate. This provides specimen-location information down to GPS accuracy, which can be a 3–5 meter radius with modern GPS. The app also provides a coordinate extent field to estimate the distance from the recorded coordinate.

### Controlled Vocabularies

Controlled vocabularies are predefined lists of terms used to ensure consistency and standardization during data entry. This is common practice in data management systems. NAHPU allows you to customize controlled vocabularies for your research group, while also providing some common terms by default. When you first install NAHPU, the setup wizard guides you through selecting and adding controlled vocabularies.

### Field Identifiers

The practice of assigning a unique identifier to a specimen in the field can be categorized into three types: personal, institutional, and project-specific field IDs. NAHPU supports personal field IDs by assigning a person the `cataloger` role. Add your initials and current field number in the personnel form, and the app will automatically generate a unique field ID by combining the cataloger's initials and field number. The number is auto-incremented and can be overridden manually. For other forms of field IDs, NAHPU uses the term `project ID`. You can set a prefix, suffix, and starting number in the specimen settings. The setup wizard guides you through the process, or you can complete it later in the specimen settings.

{% aside type="note" %}
NAHPU automatically assigns a UUID to each specimen record. This ensures that every specimen always has a unique identifier in case of a field-ID conflict, such as when two catalogers use the same initials and similar ranges of field numbers.
{% /aside %}

### Cataloger vs Collector Roles

NAHPU limits the collector role to someone who collects the specimen. This is a specific field in the specimen form. Anyone with a specimen-care role can be assigned as a collector. Your field guide who brought the specimen to you, or someone who donated an unprepared specimen to the collection, can be assigned as a collector. A person who catalogs the specimen, however, is a cataloger. This role provides access to field IDs, and a person with this role can be assigned as a cataloger, preparator, collector, or any other personnel field.

### Getting Started

{% steps %}

1. **Set up NAHPU**

    When you first install NAHPU, the setup button is visible on the home screen. If you do not see it or need to set it up later, you can access it through the menu drawer. When you select it, the setup wizard guides you through selecting a field identifier and all customizable controlled-vocabulary fields. You can also import and export them, so setup only needs to be completed once for your entire team.

2. **Create a new project**

    Use the `+` button in the bottom-right corner of the home screen and select
    `New project`. The setup wizard collects project information, offers an
    optional main taxon and field-ID setup, requests a Cataloger when personnel
    IDs are selected, and finishes with a review. The project name must be 3 to
    25 characters long.

    You can skip device setup and complete it later in
    [Settings](../usages/settings). See [Projects](../usages/projects) for the
    complete wizard and project-information import workflow.

    After the review, `Create project` opens the dashboard.

    For a new project, the app automatically generates a project UUID. We use
    [UUID version 4](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Universally_unique_identifier).
    When you import project information from another device, NAHPU retains the
    imported UUID.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    You can transfer project information and its UUID using QR or JSON export.
    This is especially useful when several people catalog the same project on
    their own devices. The QR or JSON export transfers the project information,
    including its UUID, which simplifies data merging later. See
    [Transfer a Project](../usages/export/project-transfer) for more details.

    {% /aside %}

3. **Get to know the dashboard**

    The dashboard is the main project interface. It is divided into four main
    panels.

    - **The top-left panel** is the project overview. Use `Edit` and `Export info` to manage project information. Use `Export project` when you need to transfer records and media.
    - **The top-right panel** contains personnel data. Use this panel to add and manage personnel information for the project. Personnel appear as a list in this panel after they are created.
    - **The bottom-left panel** is the taxon registry. Use this panel to add and manage taxon information for the project.
    - **The bottom-right panel** contains the record statistics. It shows the number of records added to the project. You can enter full-screen mode to see more statistics.

    {% aside type="note" %}

    On a smartphone screen, the panels are arranged in this order: **project information**, **personnel**, **taxon registry**, and **record statistics**.
    {% /aside %}

4. **Add new personnel**

    You need at least one person to take on the role of specimen-care cataloger. You can add more people with different roles.

    - **[Cataloger](../usages/personnel#cataloger)** is responsible for cataloging the specimen. Their initials and field number are used to generate the Field ID on the specimen catalog page. Their name also appears whenever a field asks for personnel, such as collecting personnel and preparator. The app does not allow you to change the cataloger role after it is created.

    - **[Preparator only](../usages/personnel#preparator-only)** is a person who helps prepare the specimen but is not involved in managing the data. Their name does not appear in the cataloger field on the specimen page, but it can appear elsewhere, such as in the `Preparator` and `Collecting personnel` fields.

    - **[None](../usages/personnel#none)** is reserved for personnel who are not directly involved in specimen care. NAHPU aims to ensure that everyone involved in the field is recorded in the field data. You can assign this role to your driver, local guides, cooks, and others. Their names appear only in the `Collecting personnel` field. If they are involved in obtaining a specimen, you can also associate them as a collector.

5. **Add a new taxon**

    You can add a taxon manually using the Add taxon button or import it from a file. The preferred formats are `.xlsx`, `.csv`, and `.tsv`. NAHPU will make a best-effort attempt to parse other file types.

    - For recognized delimited files, the app selects the delimiter by extension (`.csv` = comma, `.tsv` = tab).
    - For unrecognized extensions, auto-detect tries Excel, comma, tab, and semicolon first. If that fails, use the advanced parser override and enter a custom raw delimiter.
    - `.xlsx` has the best support; older or other Excel formats may fail.

    Your import file requires all of these columns: `class`, `order`, `family`, `genus`, and `epithet`. You can also include `scientific name`, `common name`, and `notes`. The app automatically detects supported header aliases, such as `taxonClass`, `taxonOrder`, `taxonFamily`, and `specificEpithet`. You can manually select the column type using the dropdown menu if the app does not automatically assign the correct type to each column. Other columns are ignored.

    Example taxon import table:

    ```text
    | class    | order    | family  | genus   | epithet   | scientific name   | common name        | notes |
    | -------- | -------- | ------- | ------- | --------- | ----------------- | ------------------ | ----- |
    | Mammalia | Rodentia | Muridae | Bunomys | coelestis | Bunomys coelestis | Heavenly hill rat  |       |
    | Mammalia | Rodentia | Muridae | Bunomys | penitus   | Bunomys penitus   | Inland hill rat    |       |
    ```

6. **Create a site**

    Navigate to the site page using the navigation bar. To create a new site, use the `+` button in the top-right corner of the site page. Fill in at least the `Site ID`. The app limits the Site ID to 20 characters and enforces uppercase formatting. You will refer to this ID throughout the record form whenever it asks for a site.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    We recommend creating a unique Site ID convention for your team. Here is an example for mammalogy fieldwork:

    - City, town, or village names are used as they are. For example, the city of Bogor is `BOGOR`.
    - A campsite uses the `{2-letter locality abbreviation}-CAMP{camp number}` format. For example, camp 1 on Mt. Gede-Pangrango is `GP-CAMP1`.
    - A trap line uses the `{2-letter locality abbreviation}{trapline leader initial}-L{line number}` format. For example, line 1 on Mt. Gede-Pangrango led by Heru Handika is `GPHH-L1`.
    - A pitfall line uses the `{2-letter locality abbreviation}{pitfall line leader initial}-P{line number}` format. For example, line 1 on Mt. Gede-Pangrango led by Heru Handika is `GPHH-P1`.

    {% /aside %}

7. **Add a narrative**

    This is your field journal. It is good practice to write a narrative every day. You can write about anything that happened that day. Navigate to the Narrative page using the bottom navigation bar and select `+`. Add the date, time, site, writer, and Markdown-supported narrative text. You can also attach media and search or preview entries later. See [Narrative](../usages/narrative).

8. **Add collecting events**

    A collecting event captures the details of a collecting effort, the personnel involved, and environmental data for a specific site and period. Specimen records link to a collecting event rather than directly to a site.

    To create a collecting event, use the `+` button in the top-right corner of the collecting event page. Select `site ID`, `start date`, `start time`, `end date`, and `end time`. Then fill out the rest of the form.

    The app combines the Site ID and start date to create a unique ID for each collecting event. For example, `GP-L1-March, 23 2023`. You will use this ID to refer to the collecting event in the specimen record. If you run multiple collecting events at the same site on the same day, add a suffix to distinguish them, such as `Line1-Mar 26, 2023-1`, `Line1-Mar 26, 2023-2`, `Line1-Mar 26, 2023-day`, or `Line1-Mar 26, 2023-night`.

    {% aside type="tip" %}
    Unlike sites, which you create once for each new location, we recommend creating a new collecting event for **each individual collection effort** at the same site. For example, when studying nocturnal rodents, traps are typically set the evening before and checked the following morning. In this case:

    - **Start date** = the evening the traps were set

    - **End date** = the morning the traps were checked and specimens were collected

    This approach accurately captures the trapping effort and allows you to track changes in trap numbers or personnel across events. Use the event record and project statistics to review the resulting effort and specimen counts.
    {% /aside %}

9. **Add specimen records**

    To create a specimen record, use the `+` button in the top-right corner of the specimen-record page. The specimen-record icon in the navigation bar shows the currently active format. It generates the matching format for the currently active catalog. The only difference between catalog formats is the measurement fields. After creating a new specimen record, fill in the relevant information. Repeat the process for the next specimen.

    {% aside type="note" %}
    NAHPU enables collectors to document multiple taxon groups within a single project. The `Specimen Record` icon in the navigation bar reflects the currently active format. You can identify the catalog format on the current page by observing the icon in the specimen parts. For example, a `paw` represents the mammal catalog, while a `bird head` signifies the bird catalog.

    **Steps to test:**

    - Go to Settings.
    - Select **Format** and choose a catalog format different from the current format.
    - Go back to Specimen Record.
    - Create a specimen.
    {% /aside %}

10. **Export records**

    To export records, go to the dashboard menu, indicated by the hamburger icon (three horizontal lines) in the top-left corner. You have multiple export options:

    - Create a report. Generate a species list, media, and site coordinates in a delimited format (`.csv` or `.tsv`).

    - Bundle records. Create a Darwin Core Archive, Darwin Core Data Package, or complete NAHPU Data Package. Data Packages can use TAR.GZ or ZIP. See [Bundle records](../usages/export/export-bundles).

    - Export records. Choose which records to export. This option supports delimited formats (`.csv` or `.tsv`).

11. **Back up the database**

    To back up the database, go to the dashboard menu and select `backup database`. By default, the database is exported as a SQLite file. Use the `include project data` toggle to add project data to the backup. The app generates a ZIP file containing the database and media.

    {% aside type="tip" %}

    In a remote field site, having a reliable internet connection may be challenging. Use the lightweight `JSON.GZ` project export for a small, records-only upload without media. Use the database-only `sqlite` backup for database recovery, and store a ZIP/TAR.GZ project archive locally when media or a complete project copy is needed. It is also good practice to back up across devices when multiple devices use NAHPU.

    {% /aside %}

{% /steps %}
